TI  - Active Topological Learning (ATL): A Geometric Framework for Targeted Knowledge Injection in Small Language Models
AB  - We propose Active Topological Learning (ATL), a geometric methodology for surgically inserting targeted conceptual knowledge into the embedding space of pre-trained Small Language Models (SLMs). Current fine-tuning paradigms require large datasets, significant compute, and risk degrading existing model knowledge. ATL addresses this by operating at the geometric level: detecting conceptual voids using the Quality Signal metric, measuring existing manifold structure via Angular Pre-Survey, designing a geometrically derived Micro-Corpus, and training exclusively along identified load-bearing dimensions using anisotropic gradient masking. We demonstrate that a synthetic concept with zero pre-training bias can be teleported from a random sub-word cluster to a precisely localized topological neighbourhood in the quantum computing manifold in under 40 seconds on consumer laptop hardware, without causing catastrophic forgetting in unrelated semantic regions. Subsequent natural language semantic retrieval confirms the inserted concept is discoverable by naive queries without prior knowledge of its existence. We identify a Semantic Singularity upper bound on training intensity and propose the concept of a minimum propagation radius R for bridge resolution. We suggest these findings raise open questions about just-in-time knowledge injection and the feasibility of on-demand model specialisation in resource-constrained deployments, ultimately proposing that the SLM serves best as a fluid topological substrate paving the way for deterministic external graph routing.
