TI  - Adaptive Switching Between Self-Learning and Collaborative Learning in Self-and-Mutual Models: A Unified Framework for Autonomous Knowledge Acquisition
AB  - Learning systems can acquire knowledge through independent learning or collaborative learning. Independent learning enables a system to explore its own experiences, develop specialized knowledge, and preserve autonomy, whereas collaborative learning enables multiple learning entities to exchange information, resolve knowledge gaps, and benefit from complementary capabilities. However, a system that consistently relies on only one learning mode may suffer from insufficient knowledge diversity, unnecessary communication costs, redundant learning, or limited adaptability to changing tasks. This paper proposes an Adaptive Self-and-Mutual Learning Framework (ASMLF), a conceptual framework for dynamically switching between self-learning and collaborative learning within a proposed class of Self-and-Mutual Models. Unlike approaches that focus exclusively on individual learning, social learning, multi-agent coordination, or the collaboration of large language models, the proposed framework treats self-models and mutual models as distinct model-level structures that support autonomous and relational knowledge acquisition. A state-aware switching mechanism estimates learning uncertainty, knowledge deficiencies, expected collaborative gains, communication costs, and the reliability of shared knowledge to determine whether learning should proceed independently, collaboratively, or through a hybrid sequence. The framework also incorporates reciprocal knowledge transfer, collaboration-triggered self-learning, self-learning-triggered collaboration, and feedback-driven adaptation of switching policies. We formulate the framework mathematically, establish testable research propositions, and design an experimental protocol for evaluating learning performance, sample efficiency, knowledge retention, adaptability, and computational efficiency. The paper provides a conceptual foundation for investigating adaptive learning-mode selection in self-and-mutual model architectures without assuming that either learning mode is universally superior.
